{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1514102-spirit-summoning-ritual-questions","id":1514102,"title":"Spirit summoning ritual questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-07T03:13:06-07:00","id":1514102,"title":"Spirit summoning ritual questions","content":"Now that two of my players got access to the ritual I get regularly questions when they want to know more about a specific spirit. How do you rule what type of spirit they summon ? Can they summon any spirit they like without even having encountered one ? Also, the question I find hardest to answer every time is what level of power can he summon for a specific spirit. For example can he summon an ancestor-spirit that is an incarna, same about a Banshee etc. How do you determine what type of spirit got an incarna, or not ? I know that &quot;popularity of it's concept&quot; is a good way to start, but I was wondering if you have more criteria.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T04:21:12-07:00","id":1514106,"title":"","content":"I found the banshee in the fianna tribebook and some more information on rules to create a spirit in axis mundis.<br /> From that I understand that a specific spirit has a specific strength and that's it more or less. For exemple Kelpie spirits have fixed stats, and since in the white howler tribebook it is said that heir incarna died a while ago we can imagine that it was the waterhorse incana (there are a lot of different waterhorse variation in myth an legends especialy from Ireland &amp; Scotland).<br /> <br /> Now how do you think to classify ancestor spirits, they are always sa bit difficult for me. They are not the person that they represent, being instead a sort of memory of that werewolf/person. Do you think they are all more or less as powerful or there could be an incarna for ancestor spirits ? If yes, how to you imagine it ?<br /> <br /> PS : sorry for the double post, I could have edited the previous one","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-07T12:45:12-07:00","id":1514125,"title":"","content":"The Rite of Summoning and summoning spirits should be a routine part of the game. So whatever you decide, it should be done in mind to keep things easy for you to adjudicate. If it gets to be too much work, simplify things for yourself.<br /> <br /> In terms of mechanics, Rites are meant to be very generic. There can be lots of variations of each Rite, but that is handled by the Rituals roll. So a broad application is preferable.<br /> <br /> This is how I handle it:<br /> <br /> Players can summon an individual spirit if they know of one, or they can summon any generic member of that spirit type. That doesn't change the Difficulty. It's just the difference between someone phone calling a specific individual at a company by their direct line, or calling the general number of a company and getting the receptionist who will route you to somebody, or being put in a queue and whoever picks up the line is who you get. I would begin adjusting that only if the PC begins making chiminage with a specific spirit that builds a long time relationship, and then I'd use roleplay more than random dice rolling to determine how things go.<br /> <br /> In terms of what kind of spirits do PCs know - and therefore can call - that is something the ST needs to determine separately from the mechanics of the Rite of Summoning. Every ST will come to different conclusions based on what is fun for their group, what they want to see in their game, and what seems &quot;realistic&quot; for them. There is no right answer in a generic sense. There is only what seems right to you, as the ST. And that is going to be different for every ST.<br /> <br /> Some STs will decide the PC can know every kind of spirit out there, and may just let them access the sourcebooks OOC. Or they limit it to the player's existing knowledge, or what the ST wants to provide them after the PC asks &quot;I want to summon a spirit so I can do X, what kind of spirits can I summon to do that?&quot;. Others will be more restrictive to varying degrees. Any answer can be a burden or a blessing to the ST. Either the ST needs to decide on the fly what kind of spirits, or they are at the mercy of the PC summoning any spirit even if it makes no sense in the setting.<br /> <br /> I make certain assumptions on the kinds of spirits characters know.<br /> <br /> First, there are probably certain kind of spirits all Garou know. Lunes, wolf spirits, any general nature spirit you think are ubiquitous, especially if is mentioned they teach common Gifts. Raven spirits, for example, are very common and some form of them appear in many spirit broods. Just assume this was part of any generic Garou's education as a cub.<br /> <br /> Second, characters will likely know the major or general spirits of their tribal Incarna's spirit brood. Bone Gnawers will know spirits under Rat. Fianna will know spirits under Stag. And so on. These specific spirits are taught to members of those tribes. For example, you may decide that deer spirits are fairly universal, but only the Fianna would know of spirits like Yale, Hind, Black Stags, or White Harts. You might also decide that while others might only summon a generic &quot;deer spirit&quot;, that Fianna would know how to summon specifically &quot;Red Deer&quot;, &quot;Roe Deer&quot;, &quot;White Tailed Deer&quot;, &quot;Mule Deer&quot;, &quot;Elk&quot;, &quot;Reindeer&quot;, and so on. All Garou know raven spirits, but only the Get of Fenris know &quot;Hrafn&quot; while Shadow Lords know about the &quot;rogue ravens&quot;, the White Howlers knew about &quot;Carrion Bird&quot;, and so on. Also keep in mind, that certain spirits might be known to multiple tribes. A spirit like Reindeer/Caribou might be known to Fianna, Wendigo, and Get of Fenris and even more tribes.<br /> <br /> Third, that extends to Ancestors spirits. Whatever major Ancestor spirit figures (the major heroes of the tribe) will be known. Lesser Ancestor spirits that are part of the Garou's line might be known to that specific Garou (or other descendants), but not to the general population. Characters who have the Ancestors background probably know even more. (In practice, you probably want to keep Ancestor spirits as generic as possible, but give them a distinct name and personality, a brief synopsis/biography, and perhaps a specialization of some kind.) Third, characters will be familiar with any nearby spirits that are associated with their caern and caern totem.<br /> <br /> Fourth, Theurges will know more than others. They may be familiar with different kinds of Moon spirits, Plant Spirits, Elementals, etc. Again, assume this is part of their normal Theurge training, and that Theurges are continuing their studies &quot;behind the scenes&quot;. I would also extend that members of the Uktena tribe since spirit summoning is an important part of their tribal heritage. And if for some reason, you have non-Theurges making an effort to learn about the spirit world, using that rite a lot, having an unusual background, etc., then assume they have some additional knowledge as well.<br /> <br /> Fifth, you probably want certain spirits to be more mysterious or even unknown to the Garou. A good rule of thumb is how hostile corresponding Changing Breeds are, or how hostile those spirits are supposed to be. Breeds like Bastet, Mokole, Nagah, etc. are generally distant if not hostile. So various kinds of cat spirits, snake and reptile spirits, etc. are probably not well known unless there are specific exemptions (Uktena should know a lot of snake spirits). Unusual spirits like those of the oceans and waters are probably also very mysterious. Outside of the Glasswalker, tribes are probably unfamiliar with most Weaver spirits or artificial elemental spirits. Though here you may want the most basic kind of spirits to be known (like a Pattern Spider). Wyld and Wyrm spirits fall under as well.<br /> <br /> So come up with some categories that make sense to you. Discuss them with your players as a whole, and figure out what works for your gaming group.<br /> <br /> As for how many Incarna might exist, my recommendation is not to worry too much about it. Summoning an Incarna has a very high difficulty, which means even if it is successful, it is likely the Incarna will be hostile. What ritemaster wants to deal with that? Also, I believe it is written somewhere that Incarnas are only summoned if the Incarna wants to be summoned. So just say it fails regardless of how many successes a PC rolls if you don't want to deal with it. (And if I am misremembering, I still think that is a good rule.) My rule of thumb is that the only &quot;safe&quot; Incarna that can be summoned are those of your tribe, your pack totem, or one of the very few very friendly spirits to the Garou (maybe just the Wolf Incarna and the Raven Incarna). Don't try summoning any other Incarna.<br /> <br /> If you want a more comprehensive list, I would stick with the tribal Incarna, an Incarna for each of the spirits behind one of the Changing Breeds (the Bastet get the King of All Cats, but each of the Bastet breed types should also have an Incarna for them - Cougar, Lion, Tiger, Cheetah, etc.) and any of the major pack totems found in the Corebook and any other ones you really like.<br /> <br /> I also remember that you once read my thread on &quot;Additional Spirit Broods&quot;, so you may want to use that for some inspiration. One of the reasons for working on that was to answer very similar questions as this one.<br /> <br /> And of course, there are a variety of &quot;lesser Incarna&quot; who either have small broods of their own, or are part of a larger brood under a greater Incarna. Per Axis Mundi, Rabbit is part of Stag's brood, and I think it is likely that there is a Rabbit Incarna as well.<br /> <br /> I would not say there is an Incarna of Ancestor spirits, because Ancestor spirits always represent a distinct &quot;hero&quot;/ancestor. They may or may not be the actual &quot;ghost&quot; or &quot;soul&quot; or &quot;spirit stuff&quot; of that specific ancestor, but they are specific to it. In so far there is an &quot;Incarna&quot; of the Ancestor spirits, I would make it the &quot;Progenitor Wolf&quot; of Silver Fang legendry who saved Gaia from Death.<br /> <br /> But however complicated you make this, you should balance your needs for the game, how simple you need it to run, with how fun it is for you to figure this stuff out. This is probably more in depth than 95%+ of players ever need. Focus on what you need for your games and something easy for you to adjudicate while still making sense to your game group. Once you have a few ideas, share them with your gaming group, and you can probably work something out that isn't too difficult.<br /> <br /> And I would put a lot of burden on coming up with any individual spirits on the player(s) that want to do this. If they want to summon a kelpie spirit, have them provide you (as ST) an out-of-character list of names for such spirits (maybe 3-5) or any other detail you need (like appearance or personality). You can always change them as needed and pick one at random whenever one is summoned. The burden should not always be on you. And this also gives you a heads up on what kind of spirits they want to summon. Soon you'll have a &quot;library&quot; of such spirits that you can keep going back to as needed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":".  typos 06-08-2023, 04:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-08T05:06:12-07:00","id":1514184,"title":"","content":"Thank you for this very detailed answer there are a few takes that I took myself without thinking too much about it. Since our game is a game by forum there are a lot of things that I do my myself but it's a very good idea to involve my players more in the process.<br /> <br /> The player interested about this have a spiritual heritage with ancestors spirit at 5 dots, hence why he's feeling confident about not dying in the process. But yeah overall I was doubtful there would be a incarna for ancestor spirit since they represent people, your idea is interesting though. <br /> <br /> Anyway thank you, I was always a bit conflicted about ancestor-spirit, this will help me regarding that topic and the use of the ritual by extension.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}